 "critic": "Three families of cards read as generated. The Claws-fee cards are one identical sentence with only names swapped, and they end on a vague 'Can you do something about it?' that leaves the player with no clear action. The short bounty cards (lost item, tail, missing person, escort, courier, convince a gang member) are a bare task plus a round price, with no reason attached. The long messages follow one skeleton: an incident clause, a first-person plea, a three-option list ('X, Y, or Z'), then a stock line about the player's reputation or being a stranger. The incident clauses and closing lines are interchangeable parts. Several long cards also contradict themselves: 'I had never seen them before' sits beside a named assailant, and 'nobody on the row had seen the thief' sits beside a named thief. The Ivo Popov card stands out as written for its situation. It has a specific deadline, a named collector, a consequence ('someone less patient'), and local texture (the stalls starting to say no among themselves)."
